# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. `.env.local` is gitignored; this file is not,
# so never put a real value here.
#
# Anything named EXPO_PUBLIC_* is compiled into the app bundle and readable by
# anyone who downloads it. Everything else is server-only and stays on the
# machine running the API routes. That split is load-bearing — see SECURITY.md.

# ── Required: the model behind Aria ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-only. Without it every route silently falls back to its offline path:
# chat still parses dates, assignments still break down, drafts still write —
# all from scripted logic rather than from Claude. The app looks like it works,
# which is exactly what makes a missing key easy to miss.
#
# Get one at https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
# Real keys start `sk-ant-api03-` and run to about 108 characters. A short
# value is a placeholder and will 401.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-api03-...

# ── Required: Supabase ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both are public by design. The anon key is meant to ship in the bundle; row
# level security is what actually protects the data, not the secrecy of this
# key. Never put the service_role key here — it bypasses RLS entirely.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# ── Optional: sending email on the user's behalf ────────────────────────────
# Server-only. Without these, Aria drafts and addresses an email but can't send
# it — the UI says so rather than pretending. https://resend.com/api-keys
RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
ARIA_FROM_EMAIL=aria@yourdomain.com

# ── Optional: hardening ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Require a confirmed email before any API route will serve a request. Off
# during testing because unconfirmed accounts are the ones you're testing with.
ARIA_REQUIRE_CONFIRMED_EMAIL=0

# Refuse to boot when a required variable is missing, instead of degrading
# quietly. Worth setting to 1 in production — the quiet degradation above is
# helpful in development and dangerous in a release.
ARIA_STRICT_CONFIG=0

# ── Optional: rate limits (per hour) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults are in src/lib/rate-limit.ts. Raise deliberately: the per-user cap
# bounds one account, the global cap bounds your bill.
# ARIA_AI_PER_USER_HOURLY=30
# ARIA_AI_GLOBAL_HOURLY=500
# ARIA_MAIL_PER_USER_HOURLY=10
# ARIA_MAIL_GLOBAL_HOURLY=100
